WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.119
It's time for us to walk through the orchestrator role in multi-agent systems.

2
00:00:05.119 --> 00:00:09.319
Over the next few minutes, we'll look at the three core functions every orchestrator

3
00:00:09.319 --> 00:00:16.100
performs, how task assignment and result aggregation works, what the orchestrator retains versus

4
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:21.680
what it delegates, and how to design for observability and error handling.

5
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.760
Strip an orchestrator down and it's really doing one job, holding the plan that no single

6
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.479
sub-agent can see.

7
00:00:28.479 --> 00:00:33.479
It takes a high-level goal and decomposes it, which means breaking it into pieces small

8
00:00:33.479 --> 00:00:35.040
enough to delegate.

9
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.599
The word that matters there is bounded.

10
00:00:37.599 --> 00:00:44.400
Each sub-task needs a clear start and a clear end so the sub-agent knows what finished looks like.

11
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.779
Then it assigns those pieces, and when the work comes back it aggregates, collecting

12
00:00:48.779 --> 00:00:55.040
the outputs, checking them, and synthesizing one coherent answer to the original request.

13
00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:01.400
Decomposition, assignment, and aggregation are the full life cycle of orchestrated execution,

14
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.720
from goal intake to final delivery.

15
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.559
Everything else in this lesson hangs off those three.

16
00:01:08.559 --> 00:01:12.639
Every orchestrator performs three core functions, and they happen in sequence.

17
00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:14.360
First, decompose.

18
00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:21.199
The orchestrator takes the high-level goal and breaks it into sub-tasks with bounded scope. Second, assign.

19
00:01:21.199 --> 00:01:26.160
The orchestrator routes each sub-task to the appropriate sub-agent or tool based on

20
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.160
what that sub-task requires.

21
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:30.040
Third, aggregate.

22
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.760
The orchestrator collects what comes back, validates it, and synthesizes it.

23
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:39.400
What's important to recognize is that none of these functions happen automatically.

24
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.559
The orchestrator has to be explicitly designed to perform each one.

25
00:01:43.559 --> 00:01:48.319
If any of these three functions is missing or poorly defined, the multi-agent system

26
00:01:48.319 --> 00:01:52.760
will fail to produce reliable output at scale.

27
00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.559
Effective task assignment starts with scoping.

28
00:01:55.559 --> 00:02:00.639
Before you can assign a sub-task, it needs a clear, narrow definition.

29
00:02:00.639 --> 00:02:02.400
What does success look like?

30
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.000
What tools or context are needed?

31
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.120
What's the expected output format?

32
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.000
The orchestrator holds all of this routing logic.

33
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.919
Sub-agents don't self-select their work.

34
00:02:12.919 --> 00:02:15.419
They receive assignments with instructions.

35
00:02:15.419 --> 00:02:20.380
The most common cause of sub-agent failure isn't the sub-agent itself, but a poorly

36
00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:26.380
scoped sub-task arriving from an orchestrator that didn't define the work clearly enough.

37
00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:31.460
Good task assignment is as much about precision in decomposition as it is about matching the

38
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.460
right agent to the right job.

39
00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:38.539
One of the most important things to understand about orchestration is what the orchestrator

40
00:02:38.539 --> 00:02:41.600
retains versus what it delegates.

41
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:47.080
The orchestrator keeps the master plan, the execution state that tracks what's been completed

42
00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:52.880
and what's still in progress, the error handling logic for when things go wrong, and the aggregation

43
00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.520
rules for how outputs get combined.

44
00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:02.759
Sub-agents, by contrast, hold only what the orchestrator explicitly provides, their instructions,

45
00:03:02.759 --> 00:03:07.800
the context for their specific sub-task, and the tools they need to complete it.

46
00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.759
Nothing is inherited automatically.

47
00:03:09.759 --> 00:03:15.160
This separation is intentional, and it's fundamental to how multi-agent systems maintain

48
00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:20.360
predictability and avoid cross-contamination between tasks.

49
00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:24.399
Result aggregation happens in three steps. First, collect.

50
00:03:24.399 --> 00:03:29.479
The orchestrator gathers outputs from all sub-agents once they complete their assigned work.

51
00:03:29.479 --> 00:03:31.320
Second, validate.

52
00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:38.080
Each output is checked against expected format, schema, or constraints before it's used in synthesis.

53
00:03:38.199 --> 00:03:42.800
Skipping validation is a very common mistake that causes downstream errors, which are hard

54
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.000
to trace back to their source.

55
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.199
Third, synthesize.

56
00:03:47.199 --> 00:03:52.039
Validated outputs are combined into a coherent result that actually answers the original

57
00:03:52.039 --> 00:03:53.500
high-level goal.

58
00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:58.479
The orchestrator is the only component with visibility across all sub-task results, which

59
00:03:58.479 --> 00:04:06.960
is exactly why aggregation is centralized there, rather than distributed across individual sub-agents.

60
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.800
When a sub-agent fails, the orchestrator has three options. Retry.

61
00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:19.119
Reissue the same task with the same or slightly adjusted instructions if the failure looks transient. Substitute.

62
00:04:19.119 --> 00:04:26.880
Route the task to an alternative agent or tool if the original sub-agent is unavailable or unsuitable. Escalate.

63
00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:33.739
Surface the failure to a human for intervention when neither retry nor substitution is appropriate.

64
00:04:33.739 --> 00:04:41.179
The key design principle here is that error-handling logic belongs in the orchestrator, not in the sub-agents.

65
00:04:41.179 --> 00:04:44.500
Sub-agents shouldn't be making decisions about what to do when they fail.

66
00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.059
They report the failure.

67
00:04:46.059 --> 00:04:49.059
The orchestrator decides how to respond.

68
00:04:49.059 --> 00:04:54.000
That separation keeps the system's behavior predictable and auditable.

69
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.019
The orchestrator pattern maps directly onto what's called the hub-and-spoke topology.

70
00:04:59.019 --> 00:05:00.980
The orchestrator is the hub.

71
00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:07.100
All task assignments, all result collection, and all routing decisions flow through it.

72
00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:08.380
Sub-agents are the spokes.

73
00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:12.299
They receive work from the hub and return results to the hub.

74
00:05:12.299 --> 00:05:14.940
Spokes don't communicate with each other directly.

75
00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.019
Everything is mediated by the hub.

76
00:05:17.019 --> 00:05:21.260
This centralization is what makes the system easy to reason about and audit.

77
00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:25.579
But it comes with a real trade-off, that the exam tests explicitly.

78
00:05:25.579 --> 00:05:29.899
Hub-and-spoke creates a single point of failure and a potential bottleneck.

79
00:05:29.899 --> 00:05:34.700
If the orchestrator fails or becomes overloaded, the entire system stalls.

80
00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:39.140
That's the architectural cost of centralized coordination.

81
00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:44.179
An orchestrator that can't explain its decisions is very difficult to debug.

82
00:05:44.179 --> 00:05:50.579
Observability means designing the orchestrator to log what it's doing and why at each stage.

83
00:05:50.579 --> 00:05:55.619
That includes logging every task assignment with the routing rationale behind it, recording

84
00:05:55.619 --> 00:06:01.899
progress state after each sub-agent, complete so you know exactly where execution is at

85
00:06:01.899 --> 00:06:07.500
any point, and capturing error events with the context that triggered them.

86
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:12.920
When these logs exist, the orchestrator's decision chain becomes an audit trail.

87
00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:18.500
You can trace back through any execution and understand exactly what happened and why.

88
00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:24.660
Without that logging, debugging a multi-agent system often means rerunning the entire pipeline

89
00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:28.739
from scratch to reproduce an issue.

90
00:06:28.739 --> 00:06:34.019
An orchestrator decomposes a goal, routes each piece, and stitches what comes back into

91
00:06:34.019 --> 00:06:35.899
a single answer.

92
00:06:35.899 --> 00:06:39.579
It's the only component with a view across the entire run.

93
00:06:39.579 --> 00:06:45.579
That's why the retry or escalate call belongs there and not inside a sub-agent.

94
00:06:45.579 --> 00:06:50.480
Keep in mind, centralizing every routing decision also concentrates the failure.

95
00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:55.059
If the hub stalls, everything behind it stalls as well.

96
00:06:55.059 --> 00:06:56.339
That wraps up this lesson.

97
00:06:56.339 --> 00:06:57.500
I'll see you in the next one.

